WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.870
Human beings are absolute
pattern detectors.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.804
We spot patterns and we spot
structures.

00:00:06.804 --> 00:00:08.250
So you can say, look,

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.730
here is this beautiful pattern,
and underneath that pattern,

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.250
there is this wonderful mathematical idea.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.960
Part of the reason we find it beautiful
is because of its inherent symmetry in

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:19.720
proportion.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.520
If you understand
how something is put together,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.870
if you can see that behind that
beautiful form of poetry, art,

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.320
and music and literature,
the beauty of architecture,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.640
those shapes that nature produces,
that's all mathematics as well.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.370
My main research area has been group
theory. Really it's the study of symmetry,

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.556
that's how I like to think of it.

00:00:46.556 --> 00:00:49.140
A group is just a collection of things,

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.800
which could be numbers,
it could be functions,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.773
it could be matrices,

00:00:54.773 --> 00:00:56.070
all of these things can form groups.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.976
But the one I like the most is
groups of symmetries.

00:00:58.976 --> 00:01:05.040
Numbers, shapes, symmetry, all of those
things are there in the universe,

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.734
the nature around us.

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:09.450
And this one here, this beautiful flower.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.290
It's got five petals and that
we've got a fivefold symmetry.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.320
You can rotate it one fifth
of the way round the circle.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.520
You get lots of numbers like 5, 8, 13,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.510
that form part of the Fibonacci
sequence, which is very,

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.020
very useful in terms of plant growth
because it relates to something called the

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:29.006
golden ratio.

00:01:29.006 --> 00:01:32.550
If you look at a sunflower,

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.120
there are loads of spirals with
Fibonacci numbers of seeds in them.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.400
So the golden number is there for sure,

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.670
and you see it as well in some of
the spiral shapes of seashells.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.460
We have not invented
the golden ratio, right?

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.220
We've discovered it and
we see it all over nature.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.580
This is the absolute lodestone of symmetry where I live.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.930
These beautiful designs created by
William Morris with botanical themes,

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.658
plants and animals.

00:02:02.658 --> 00:02:05.610
He made fabrics,
tapestries, he made wallpapers.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.234
There are 17 different
kinds of wallpaper.

00:02:08.234 --> 00:02:12.480
Now, this seems counterintuitive because
of course there are as many kinds of

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:13.830
wallpaper as you could design.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:19.470
But what we're talking about there
is the ways that the patterns can fit together.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:22.350
These designs,
they're all different,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.160
but what they have in common is
that they all have symmetry to them.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.210
The famous artist, M.C. Escher,

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.910
designed lots of repeating
patterns and tessellations.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:37.350
He learned about the 17 wallpaper
patterns from his brother who was a

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.060
crystallographer. And then of course,

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:42.660
Escher produces all these wonderful
designs that we all know and love.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.650
This collection of structures actually has
applications beyond the design of wallpaper.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.985
For instance, crystal structures.

00:02:50.985 --> 00:02:54.810
Now, if you think of the layers of
atoms that form inside a crystal,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.112
there are these 17 possibilities.

00:02:57.112 --> 00:02:58.560
So what's a symmetry?

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.660
Something you can do to that design
that leaves it looking the same.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:05.650
There are lots of vertical mirror lines
you could draw, reflect this design,

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:07.030
it's going to look the same either way.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:12.520
You could also shift it along in
steps so you could translate it,

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.395
that's the mathematical
word.

00:03:14.395 --> 00:03:18.540
So it's very, very cool the way the symmetries
can combine like this.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:23.110
And that's one of the properties that
they must satisfy to form this algebraic

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:24.008
object called a group.

00:03:24.008 --> 00:03:28.300
So in this case, we've got a collection of all the
symmetries of this beautiful design,

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:32.440
and anytime you combine any of them,
the outcome is still another symmetry.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:39.130
Just check. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.210
There's not one connection between
mathematics and music, there are many.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:51.010
It's got so much mathematics in the
rhythms, the patterns, even the counting,

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:55.953
the way that frequencies match up to
produce sounds that we find pleasing together.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:00.340
The mathematician Leibniz said that
music is what happens when we do

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.170
mathematics unconsciously.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:07.330
You can't listen to a piece of Bach
without knowing that it's mathematical.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.570
The beautiful patterns and
symmetries in that music.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.660
If you're listening to a fugue
or playing a fugue on the piano,

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:17.110
you'll get a theme that
comes in and it comes back,

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:20.770
but it's upside down, inverted. Or
it might be retrograde backwards.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.560
Or it might be translated, up or down.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:28.382
So these are just the same kinds of
symmetries that we saw in the wallpaper, right?

00:04:28.382 --> 00:04:32.890
People, somehow, they get
put off the idea of mathematics.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:37.120
They think mathematics is about
numbers, and numbers are not creative,

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.231
they're rigid.

00:04:38.231 --> 00:04:41.271
Whereas art is
more free and creative.

00:04:41.271 --> 00:04:44.430
Actually, of course, that is not right.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.170
The most
creative subject for me is mathematics.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.608
It's just that we are creating
with ideas, with patterns.

00:04:50.608 --> 00:04:56.140
So yes, music is mathematics, just as art is
mathematics, and so is literature.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.400
Everything is mathematics.

